    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's social-media environment is shaped by the difference between a platform's formal restrictions and its actual reachability on particular networks. Reporting and technical measurements document changes in access, while also showing why a single nationwide label can conceal important differences.[s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Social media", "Messaging", "Internet filtering", "Digital rights", "Network measurement"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:27:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:27:38Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Social Media and Platform Access in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore documented changes in platform access in Iran and the differences between policy announcements, filtering, and internet shutdowns."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "access-and-use",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access is more than a platform label",
        "content": "For readers encountering Iran's digital environment, terms such as blocked, available, and popular require care. A government decision to remove a restriction does not necessarily mean that every user immediately obtains access. Conversely, blocking does not demonstrate that nobody uses a service. Associated Press reporting on the December 2024 removal of restrictions on WhatsApp and Google Play recorded differing experiences on computers and mobile phones, alongside the use of proxies and virtual private networks to reach restricted services.[s1]\n\nMessaging and public social publishing should also be distinguished. WhatsApp can carry private conversations, while a public video platform exposes material to a different audience. Grouping every service under social media can obscure what a restriction prevents: private contact, public distribution, or both.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documented-changes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented sequence of changes",
        "content": "On 24 December 2024, Associated Press reported that Iran's Supreme Council of Cyberspace had lifted restrictions on WhatsApp and Google Play. This was a specific decision concerning named services, rather than evidence that all platform restrictions had ended.[s1]\n\nThe Open Observatory of Network Interference, or OONI, subsequently examined WhatsApp connectivity. Its August 2025 finding states that measurement results corroborated the December unblocking, but also identified renewed anomalies between 15 June and 12 July 2025. OONI interpreted these results as evidence suggesting interference on some Iranian networks during and after the June conflict with Israel. It did not describe every anomalous test as definitive proof of a nationwide block.[s2]\n\nFreedom House's 2025 assessment separately described extensive website filtering and restrictions on major social platforms. Its principal reporting period ran from June 2024 through May 2025, with additional discussion of the June conflict. That period matters: an annual report's title should not be mistaken for continuous observation through the present.[s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measurement-and-consequences",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What measurements can establish",
        "content": "OONI's investigation distinguished service interference from a general loss of connectivity. Some tests established network connections but failed during the encrypted connection handshake. The researchers also compared results with WhatsApp reachability elsewhere, reducing the likelihood that a global service outage explained the Iranian anomalies. These distinctions give a more precise account than simply saying that the internet was unavailable.[s2]\n\nRestrictions also have consequences beyond loading a particular application. Freedom House documented surveillance and punishment connected to online expression during its reporting period. Technical access and freedom to speak are therefore separate questions: a person may reach a platform while facing serious consequences for using it.[s3]\n\nFor interpreting future reports, ask which service was tested, on which networks, during which dates, and with what uncertainty. A policy announcement, a technical test, and a user's experience illuminate different aspects of access. None should automatically substitute for the others.[s1][s2] A complete account of social media additionally needs evidence about everyday cultural exchange, commerce, domestic platforms, and differences among communities; the sources inspected here do not adequately cover those subjects.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
