    "description": "Persian-language podcasts include narrated nonfiction, book explanations, and accounts of lived experience. Channel B, Bplus, and Radio Marz illustrate distinct editorial approaches, although their catalogs do not establish the size or composition of the wider listening public.[s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Persian podcasts", "Audio storytelling", "Nonfiction", "Books", "Lived experience"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:27:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:27:38Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Persian Podcasts: Stories, Books and Experience | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover contrasting approaches to Persian audio through Channel B, Bplus, and Radio Marz, with guidance on sources, formats, and listening."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-listening-experiences",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different kinds of listening",
        "content": "Persian podcasts offer several ways into a subject: following a carefully constructed story, encountering the argument of a nonfiction book, or listening to accounts of social experience. These approaches are visible in the publisher descriptions and catalogs of Channel B, Bplus, and Radio Marz. They are useful examples of editorial variety, rather than a ranking or a representative census of Persian audio.[s1][s2][s3]\n\nFor an international reader, Persian-language publishing should not automatically be equated with an audience located entirely inside Iran. The inspected pages describe programs and production, but do not establish where all their listeners live. Language, subject matter, production location, and audience geography are separate questions.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "channel-b",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Channel B: constructing an audio narrative",
        "content": "Channel B's own history dates its first episode to summer 1394 in the Iranian Solar Hijri calendar. It describes an initial project telling true stories, launched with assistance from Namlik, that gradually developed into a team production. Ali Bandari is identified as its founder and narrator.[s1]\n\nThe team's description reveals work that can disappear behind a recognizable voice. Editing and sound design involve arranging recorded speech, selecting music, and shaping the audio narrative. Other responsibilities include artwork, technical maintenance, release management, and financial administration. A podcast can therefore be a collaborative editorial production even when listeners mainly recognize its narrator.[s1]\n\nChannel B also distinguishes free listening from permission to republish. Its information page says episodes are free to hear, while republication of the podcast or its text requires permission. Accessibility to listeners should not be interpreted as an unrestricted reuse license.[s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "bplus",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Bplus: introducing books and ideas",
        "content": "Bplus describes its podcast as Persian summaries of nonfiction books. Its Acast catalog credits writing, narration, and editing separately for book episodes, making the editorial stages visible. A summary is best approached as an introduction to an author's argument: selection and compression necessarily shape what the listener encounters.[s2]\n\nThe same feed also contains broader explanatory productions, including material about the Caucasus and Saudi Arabia. Some descriptions supply reading links and separate credits for script and audiovisual production. This makes the feed a useful example of the overlap between an audio program and a wider digital publishing project. Readers should inspect each episode's description to establish its format and underlying sources rather than assuming every entry is a book summary.[s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "radio-marz",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Radio Marz: experience and social distance",
        "content": "Radio Marz's catalog takes another approach, organizing episodes around experiences that create distance between people. Entries published before the editorial cutoff address job loss, online harassment of women, expectations of masculinity, and political estrangement among people close to one another. The descriptions foreground personal accounts rather than presenting these subjects as statistical surveys.[s3]\n\nThat distinction matters when listening. An account may reveal how a situation feels, what language people use to explain it, and which relationships it strains. It does not by itself establish how common that experience is across Iran. The catalog also identifies sponsors for some episodes; sponsorship disclosure is part of understanding the production context.[s3]\n\nAcross these examples, a productive starting point is to ask what an episode promises: a narrative, an explanation of a published argument, or testimony. Then examine its credits and references. The reward is a more precise understanding of both the subject and the editorial choices that brought it to the listener.[s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
